it's clear high inflation isn't going  away anytime soon but who's to blame  many are pointing the finger president  biden others at fed share jerome powell  and still others at large corporations  i'm going to explain what inflation  really is how it works and who's to  blame for the fastest price increases  we've seen in decades  let's get into it inflation simply means  that prices are being inflated or rising  and that the pace of that increase is  moving quickly this is measured by  economists who track the price of goods  and services from one month to the next  and compare it to previous periods and  thus we can see how today's inflation  stacks up inflation is a bit like a  snowball rolling downhill as prices  start to rise for some items other items  that use those items and businesses that  rely on them start to raise their prices  as well if this gets out of hand you can  start to see runaway inflation where  prices rise by hundreds or even  thousands of percentage points in a year  but that's extremely rare and generally  happens when governments and central  banks make big mistakes but even  moderately high inflation has a culprit  and in the u.s inflation is rising at an  8.6 annual rate the fastest it has been  in 40 years so who's to blame lots of  people point the finger at federal  reserve chair jerome powell legendary  economist milton friedman famously said  inflation is always and everywhere a  monetary phenomenon and it's true the  fed pumped 120 billion dollars a month  into the u.s economy for years swelling  its balance sheet to more than eight  trillion dollars and around half of that  came after march 2020 but prior to 2020  the fed had used this strategy and  inflation remained very low after 2020  though the fed kept interest rates near  zero despite trillions in stimulus  spending by the federal government they  are just now beginning to raise interest  rates which increases the cost of  borrowing and slows down the economy in  an effort to reign in price increases  but rates are still negative today when  accounting for inflation then there's  the president and the truth is the  person in the oval office deserves some  blame president joe biden signed the  american rescue plan which the  congressional budget office said would  add 1.85 trillion dollars to the u.s  budget deficit and then there's former  president donald trump who signed the  2.3 trillion cares act and the 900  billion consolidated appropriations act  in december one way to think of  inflation is too much money chasing too  few goods and by providing trillions of  dollars in money to american households  the president and congress helped tip  the scales on inflation trump also  nominated powell to his role as fed  chair and biden renominated him last  year so even if inflation is simply a  monetary phenomenon the president picks  the person in charge of monetary policy  and therefore has to take a little bit  of the blame earnings reports show that  s p 500 companies on average are raising  their prices by about 20 percent more  than the rate of inflation for wholesale  prices that means that as the prices for  their inputs have increased they've  raised the prices by a lot more their  profit margins are also at record highs  so a good bit of inflation is due to big  companies don't forget kovid 19 the war  in ukraine and other price shocks have  played a big role and finally there's us  regular working people everyday  americans push up inflation in two ways  first by demanding higher wages from  their employer putting pressure on  companies to raise prices to account for  rising labor costs and second we keep  paying higher prices if consumers stayed  home and refused to pay companies  profits would take a beating and they'd  be forced to bring down their prices  but because individuals keep spending  companies keep pushing prices up the  bottom line is no one person or entity  is entirely responsible for inflation  and in fact 